    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Interviewers for AI roles ask the big-picture question more often than juniors expect, because it reveals whether
    you have a mental model or a pile of memorized terms. “Explain machine learning to someone non-technical.” “What
    is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?” “What are the parts of a training loop?” “What
    is a hyperparameter?” These are easy marks if you have one clear picture, and that picture is the four-part loop
    on this page. Every later note, including the ones on transformers, is a specialization of it.
  </p>
  <p>Questions that are really this note:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“How is machine learning different from ordinary programming?”</li>
    <li>“Walk me through what happens when a model trains.”</li>
    <li>“Parameters versus hyperparameters?”</li>
    <li>“Is training a language model supervised or unsupervised?”</li>
    <li>“Why do we split data into train and test sets?”</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
    <p>
      The four parts, the kinds of learning that interviews mention, the standard workflow, the vocabulary, and one
      tiny model built by hand. Each part gets its own detailed note later this week and next; this note is the map.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      In ordinary programming you write the rules. To tell spam from real email you would write “if the subject
      contains FREE and the sender is unknown, it is spam”, and a hundred more rules, and still miss most of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In machine learning you do not write the rules. You collect ten thousand emails that someone has already marked
      spam or not spam, hand them to a program with a lot of adjustable dials, and let the program turn its own dials
      until its answers match the marks. The rules end up inside the dial settings. Nobody wrote them, and nobody can
      easily read them, but they work on the next ten thousand emails too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cooking is the same shape. The <em>recipe</em> is the model, with amounts you can adjust. The <em>taste test</em>
      is the loss: how far is this from what it should be? And <em>“a bit less salt next time”</em> is the optimizer.
      Cook, taste, adjust, repeat. That loop is all of machine learning.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The examples you learn from</td><td><span class="term">training data</span></td><td><Math tex="(\mathbf{x}_i, y_i)" /> pairs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The facts about one example</td><td><span class="term">features</span> (inputs)</td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{x}" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The right answer for one example</td><td><span class="term">label</span> (target)</td><td><Math tex="y" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The recipe with adjustable amounts</td><td><span class="term">model</span></td><td><Math tex="f_\theta(\mathbf{x})" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The adjustable amounts</td><td><span class="term">parameters</span> (weights)</td><td><Math tex="\theta" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The recipe’s guess</td><td><span class="term">prediction</span></td><td><Math tex="\hat{y}" /> (“y hat”)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The taste test: how wrong is the guess?</td><td><span class="term">loss function</span></td><td><Math tex="L(\hat{y}, y)" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Less salt next time”</td><td><span class="term">optimizer</span></td><td>gradient descent</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cooking it again with the new amounts</td><td><span class="term">training step</span> (iteration)</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Serving the dish to a guest</td><td><span class="term">inference</span> (prediction on new data)</td><td></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="four-parts">The four parts of every ML system</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Every machine-learning system you will ever meet has the same four parts: some <strong>data</strong> to learn
      from, a <strong>model</strong> with knobs, a <strong>loss</strong> that scores how wrong the model is, and an
      <strong>optimizer</strong> that turns the knobs to make the loss smaller. Connect them in a loop and run the
      loop many times. When you are confused by a complicated system later, find these four parts and the confusion
      usually goes away.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={1} caption="The training loop. Data goes in, the model guesses, the loss compares the guess with the label, and the optimizer nudges the model’s parameters. Repeat thousands of times.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Loop diagram: data flows into a model, which produces a prediction; the loss compares the prediction with the label; the optimizer updates the model">
      <g class="sketch">
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="30" y="110" width="120" height="70" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="230" y="110" width="140" height="70" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-green" x="470" y="110" width="120" height="70" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-blue" x="230" y="230" width="140" height="56" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="470" y="20" width="120" height="56" rx="8"></rect>
        <path class="stroke" d="M150 145 H222" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M370 145 H462" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M530 76 V102" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M530 180 V258 H378" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M300 230 V188" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d="M90 110 V48 H462" stroke-dasharray="6 5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text x="90" y="140" text-anchor="middle">data</text>
      <text class="small" x="90" y="162" text-anchor="middle">features x</text>
      <text x="300" y="140" text-anchor="middle">model</text>
      <text class="small" x="300" y="162" text-anchor="middle">f(x; θ), the knobs</text>
      <text x="530" y="140" text-anchor="middle">loss</text>
      <text class="small" x="530" y="162" text-anchor="middle">how wrong is ŷ?</text>
      <text x="300" y="253" text-anchor="middle">optimizer</text>
      <text class="small" x="300" y="274" text-anchor="middle">turn the knobs</text>
      <text class="small" x="530" y="43" text-anchor="middle">label y</text>
      <text class="small" x="530" y="62" text-anchor="middle">the right answer</text>
      <text class="small" x="414" y="138" text-anchor="middle">prediction ŷ</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="316" y="214">update θ</text>
      <text class="small" x="280" y="40">the data also carries the labels</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Take the parts one at a time, with the example this track will use for the next two weeks: predicting a house’s price from its size.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Data.</strong> A set of examples, each with features and, for supervised learning, a label. Here each
    example is one house: the feature is its size, the label is the price it sold for. The data is usually written
    as a matrix <Math tex="X" /> with one row per example (the data matrix from the
    <a href="/ai/notes/math/matrices-and-shapes/">matrices note</a>) and a vector <Math tex="\mathbf{y}" /> of labels.
    Data is the part people underestimate: in practice, more and cleaner data beats a cleverer model far more often
    than the other way round.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Model.</strong> A function with adjustable parameters that turns features into a prediction. The simplest
    one for house prices is a line, <Math tex="\hat{y} = w \cdot \text{size} + b" />, with two parameters. A
    <Term id="neural-network">neural network</Term> (week 5) is the same idea with millions of parameters and a more
    flexible shape. Choosing the <em>form</em> of the
    model (line? tree? network? how big?) is your job; choosing the parameter <em>values</em> is the optimizer’s.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Loss.</strong> A single number that says how wrong the model’s predictions are on the data, with zero
    meaning perfect. For prices, the standard choice is the <span class="term">mean squared error</span>: take each
    prediction minus its label, square it so that over- and under-predictions both count as bad, and average. The
    loss turns the vague goal “predict well” into a precise target: make this number small.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Optimizer.</strong> The procedure that changes the parameters to reduce the loss. The one everyone uses
    is <span class="term">gradient descent</span>: compute which way each parameter should move to reduce the loss by
    a little, move all of them that way, repeat. Week 2 is about exactly how. For now the key insight is that the
    optimizer never sees the problem; it only sees the loss number and the knobs, and it turns knobs to push the
    number down.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run the loop: predict on the data, compute the loss, update the parameters, predict again. Each pass through the
    whole dataset is an <span class="term">epoch</span>. Stop when the loss stops improving, or when a clock runs
    out. The parameters at the end <em>are</em> the trained model; “saving a model” means saving those numbers.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gv9_4yMHFhI"
    title="StatQuest: A Gentle Introduction to Machine Learning"
    meta="About 12 minutes. The whole picture, with the point that testing on new data is what makes a method good, which the next section is about."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Every ML system has four parts: training data, a parameterized model, a loss function that measures prediction
      error, and an optimizer that adjusts the parameters to minimize the loss. Training is iterating that loop;
      inference is running the model with the learned parameters on new inputs.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="first-model">A first model, by hand</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Three houses sold recently: 1,000 square feet for 200 thousand, 1,500 for 300, and 2,000 for 380. You want a
      rule for pricing the next one. Guess a rule, “200 per thousand square feet”, check it against the three sales,
      see how far off you are, and adjust. Do that a few times and you have trained a model, with your own head as the
      optimizer.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Write size in thousands of square feet, so the data is <Math tex="x = (1, 1.5, 2)" /> and the prices are
    <Math tex="y = (200, 300, 380)" />. The model is a line with two knobs:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\hat{y} = w\,x + b" />
  <p>The loss is the mean squared error over the three houses:</p>
  <Math block tex="L(w, b) = \frac{1}{3}\sum_{i=1}^{3} (\hat{y}_i - y_i)^2 = \frac{1}{3}\sum_{i=1}^{3} (w x_i + b - y_i)^2" />
  <p>Now play optimizer. Try three settings of the knobs and score each one:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Knobs</th><th>Predictions for x = 1, 1.5, 2</th><th>Errors (prediction − price)</th><th>Squared</th><th>Loss (mean)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><Math tex="w = 180,\ b = 0" /></td><td>180, 270, 360</td><td>−20, −30, −20</td><td>400, 900, 400</td><td><strong>566.7</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="w = 200,\ b = 0" /></td><td>200, 300, 400</td><td>0, 0, +20</td><td>0, 0, 400</td><td><strong>133.3</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><Math tex="w = 190,\ b = 10" /></td><td>200, 295, 390</td><td>0, −5, +10</td><td>0, 25, 100</td><td><strong>41.7</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Figure n={2} caption="Three candidate lines through three houses. The loss is the average squared vertical gap between the line and the dots. The red line has the smallest gaps.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot of three houses with three candidate regression lines and their losses">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${fit.x} ${sy(fit, 0)} H${fit.x + fit.w} M${sx(fit, 0.5)} ${fit.y} V${fit.y + fit.h}`}></path>
        {candidates.map((c) => <path class={c.cls} d={line(fit, c.w, c.b)} stroke-width={c.cls.includes('red') ? 3 : 2} stroke-dasharray={c.dash || undefined} />)}
        {houses.map((h) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(fit, h.x)} cy={sy(fit, h.y)} r="7" />)}
        {houses.map((h) => <path class="stroke ink-red" d={`M${sx(fit, h.x)} ${sy(fit, h.y)} V${sy(fit, 190 * h.x + 10)}`} stroke-width="2" />)}
      </g>
      {houses.map((h) => <text class="small" x={sx(fit, h.x) + 10} y={sy(fit, h.y) + 18}>({h.x}, {h.y})</text>)}
      <text class="small" x={sx(fit, 2.5)} y={sy(fit, 0) + 20} text-anchor="end">size (thousand sq ft)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(fit, 0.5) - 6} y={fit.y - 8} text-anchor="end">price</text>
      {candidates.map((c, i) => (
        <text class={`small ${c.cls.includes('red') ? 'label-red' : ''}`} x="470" y={110 + i * 30}>{c.label}</text>
      ))}
      <text class="small label-red" x="470" y="220">red ticks = the errors</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="470" y="242">loss = mean of their squares</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    The third setting is best of the three, and you found it the way every optimizer does: by looking at how wrong
    the current guess was and moving the knobs in the direction that reduces the error. The second row was too high
    for the big house, so you lowered the slope a little and lifted the intercept to keep the small houses right.
    Gradient descent will do exactly this reasoning with calculus instead of intuition, and the
    <a href="#code">code section</a> does it with brute force. The best line of all turns out to be
    <Math tex="w = 180,\ b \approx 23.3" />, with a loss of about 22.2; you will derive that in the
    <a href="/ai/notes/ml/linear-regression/">linear regression note</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice what “learning” meant here. Nothing mystical: a model shape, a score, and a search over the knobs. The
    model never “understood” houses. It found two numbers that make a score small. Keep this deflationary picture; it
    is accurate all the way up to the largest language model, and it is what interviewers want to hear.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Training means searching over the model’s parameters for values that minimize the loss on the training data.
      For a line fit by mean squared error, the loss is the average squared residual, and the optimizer adjusts slope
      and intercept to shrink it.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="generalization">The real goal: new data</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A student who memorizes last year’s exam paper scores perfectly on last year’s paper and fails this year’s. A
      student who learned the subject does well on both. We do not care how well a model does on the houses it was
      trained on; we already know those prices. We care about the <em>next</em> house. So the only honest test of a
      model is on data it has never seen, and that is why every ML project hides some of its data from the model
      from the very start.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={3} caption="Two models fit to the same nine points. Left: a wiggly curve passing through every point has zero training loss and predicts the new point (open circle) badly. Right: a straight line misses the training points slightly and predicts the new point well.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 300" role="img" aria-label="Two scatter plots: a curve that passes exactly through every training point versus a straight line that fits the trend; a new test point is far from the curve but close to the line">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${gen.x} ${sy(gen, 0)} H${gen.x + gen.w} M${sx(gen, 0)} ${gen.y} V${gen.y + gen.h}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={wiggle(gen)} stroke-width="2.5"></path>
        {pts.map((pt) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(gen, pt.x)} cy={sy(gen, pt.y)} r="5" style="fill: var(--ink)" />)}
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(gen, newPoint.x)} cy={sy(gen, newPoint.y)} r="6"></circle>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${gen2.x} ${sy(gen2, 0)} H${gen2.x + gen2.w} M${sx(gen2, 0)} ${gen2.y} V${gen2.y + gen2.h}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={line(gen2, 0.8, 1)} stroke-width="2.5"></path>
        {pts.map((pt) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(gen2, pt.x)} cy={sy(gen2, pt.y)} r="5" style="fill: var(--ink)" />)}
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(gen2, newPoint.x)} cy={sy(gen2, newPoint.y)} r="6"></circle>
      </g>
      <text x={gen.x + gen.w / 2} y="280" text-anchor="middle">memorized: training loss 0</text>
      <text x={gen2.x + gen2.w / 2} y="280" text-anchor="middle">learned: training loss small, not 0</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(gen, newPoint.x) + 10} y={sy(gen, newPoint.y) + 4}>new point</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(gen2, newPoint.x) + 10} y={sy(gen2, newPoint.y) + 4}>new point</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    The ability to do well on unseen data is called <span class="term">generalization</span>, and it is the actual
    objective of machine learning. The training loss is only a proxy for it. A model that drives training loss to
    zero by memorizing the examples, like the wiggly curve, has <span class="term">overfit</span>: it learned the
    noise along with the pattern. A model too simple to capture the pattern at all has
    <span class="term">underfit</span>. The whole craft of ML sits between those two failures, and the
    <a href="/ai/notes/ml/overfitting-bias-variance/">overfitting note</a> next week gives it a full treatment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical rule follows immediately. Before you train anything, split the data: a <strong>training set</strong>
    the model learns from and a <strong>test set</strong> it never sees until the very end, when you measure it once.
    Most projects add a third <strong>validation set</strong> for making choices during development, so that the test
    set stays untouched. Any number you report from data the model trained on is not evidence of anything.
    The <a href="/ai/notes/ml/data-splits-cross-validation/">data splits note</a> in week 3 has the details and the
    traps.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/generalization"
    title="Google Machine Learning Crash Course: Generalization"
    meta="A short page with the same picture as Fig. 3 and a clear statement of the train/test rule. Five minutes."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “The goal is generalization, performance on data the model has not seen, so we hold out a test set from the
      start and never train or tune on it. Low training loss with high test loss is overfitting; high loss on both is
      underfitting.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="kinds">Kinds of learning</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The difference between the kinds of machine learning is mostly about <em>what the labels are</em>. Sometimes a
      human has written the right answer next to every example. Sometimes there are no right answers and you want
      the computer to find structure on its own. Sometimes the right answer is hiding inside the data itself, as when
      the next word of a sentence is the label for the words before it. And sometimes the answer arrives late, as a
      reward after a sequence of moves. Four situations, four names.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={4} caption="The kinds of learning, split by where the label comes from. Interviews mostly ask about the first two; the way language models are trained is the third.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 300" role="img" aria-label="Tree diagram: machine learning splits into supervised (regression, classification), unsupervised (clustering, dimensionality reduction), self-supervised (next-token prediction) and reinforcement learning">
      <g class="sketch">
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="290" y="20" width="160" height="46" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-green" x="20" y="120" width="160" height="46" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-blue" x="200" y="120" width="160" height="46" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="380" y="120" width="160" height="46" rx="8"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="560" y="120" width="160" height="46" rx="8"></rect>
        <path class="stroke" d="M370 66 V92 H100 V112 M370 92 H640 V112 M280 92 V112 M460 92 V112" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text x="370" y="48" text-anchor="middle">machine learning</text>
      <text x="100" y="148" text-anchor="middle">supervised</text>
      <text x="280" y="148" text-anchor="middle">unsupervised</text>
      <text x="460" y="148" text-anchor="middle">self-supervised</text>
      <text x="640" y="148" text-anchor="middle">reinforcement</text>
      <text class="small" x="100" y="192" text-anchor="middle">labels given by people</text>
      <text class="small" x="100" y="214" text-anchor="middle">regression: a number</text>
      <text class="small" x="100" y="236" text-anchor="middle">classification: a category</text>
      <text class="small" x="280" y="192" text-anchor="middle">no labels</text>
      <text class="small" x="280" y="214" text-anchor="middle">clustering (k-means)</text>
      <text class="small" x="280" y="236" text-anchor="middle">dimensionality reduction (PCA)</text>
      <text class="small" x="460" y="192" text-anchor="middle">labels made from the data</text>
      <text class="small" x="460" y="214" text-anchor="middle">predict the next word</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="460" y="236" text-anchor="middle">how language models learn</text>
      <text class="small" x="640" y="192" text-anchor="middle">rewards, not labels</text>
      <text class="small" x="640" y="214" text-anchor="middle">games, robots</text>
      <text class="small" x="640" y="236" text-anchor="middle">teaching chat models manners</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Kind</th><th>What the data looks like</th><th>Typical tasks</th><th>In these notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="term">Supervised</span></td>
          <td>Features plus a human-provided label for each example</td>
          <td><span class="term">Regression</span> predicts a number (price, temperature). <span class="term">Classification</span> predicts a category (spam or not, which digit).</td>
          <td>Linear and logistic regression, trees, most of weeks 1–3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="term">Unsupervised</span></td>
          <td>Features only, no labels</td>
          <td><span class="term">Clustering</span> groups similar examples. <span class="term">Dimensionality reduction</span> compresses features while keeping structure.</td>
          <td>k-means and PCA, week 4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="term">Self-supervised</span></td>
          <td>Raw data, with labels manufactured from it</td>
          <td>Predict a hidden part from the visible part: the next word, a hidden word, a missing patch of an image.</td>
          <td>How language models are trained, weeks 7–9</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="term">Reinforcement</span></td>
          <td>An agent acting in an environment, receiving rewards</td>
          <td>Learn behaviour that collects the most reward over time: games, robots, and teaching chat models which answers people prefer.</td>
          <td>Preference tuning of chat models, week 10, at the depth interviews need</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two clarifications that interviewers like. First, regression and classification differ only in the type of the
    label, and that changes the loss: squared error for numbers, and a different loss for categories that you meet
    next week (the <Term id="log-loss">log loss</Term>). The rest of the loop is identical. Second, a word you will
    hear constantly: a <span class="term">language model</span> is a model whose only job is to guess the next piece
    of text given the text so far, and the pieces of text it works with, usually words or parts of words, are called
    <span class="term">tokens</span>. “Large language model” (LLM) just means one with billions of parameters. So the
    question “is training an LLM supervised?” has a precise answer: the mechanics are supervised (there is a label and
    a loss at every position), but the labels cost nothing because they are the next tokens of the text itself. That
    is what “self-supervised” means, and it is why LLMs could be trained on the whole internet without anyone
    labelling it.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/glossary"
    title="Google Machine Learning Glossary"
    meta="Bookmark it. When any term in these notes is unclear, its entry here is short, correct and has an example. Look up “supervised”, “unsupervised” and “self-supervised” now."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Supervised learning uses labelled examples, with regression for numeric targets and classification for
      categorical ones. Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels, such as clusters or low-dimensional
      representations. Language-model training is self-supervised: the next token serves as the label, so the loss
      is an ordinary supervised loss but the labels are free.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="workflow">The workflow around the loop</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The training loop is the engine, but a real project is a whole car around it. Before the loop runs you have to
      decide what “good” means and get data you trust. After it runs you have to check the result on data the model
      never saw, compare it with the dumbest possible alternative, and only then let it loose on real users, where
      you keep watching it. Skipping any of these steps is how models that look great in a notebook fail in
      production.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={5} caption="The standard workflow. The training loop of Fig. 1 is only the fourth box. Evaluation always uses data that was set aside before training began." wide>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 900 170" role="img" aria-label="Seven boxes in a row: define the task and metric, get and split data, baseline, train, evaluate on held-out data, iterate, deploy and monitor; with an arrow looping back from evaluate to train">
      <g class="sketch">
        {['define task', 'get + split data', 'baseline', 'train', 'evaluate', 'iterate', 'deploy + monitor'].map((_, i) => (
          <rect class={`stroke ${i === 3 ? 'fill-gold' : i === 4 ? 'fill-green' : 'fill-paper'}`} x={20 + i * 125} y="50" width="110" height="56" rx="8" />
        ))}
        {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((i) => <path class="stroke" d={`M${130 + i * 125} 78 H${137 + i * 125}`} marker-end="url(#arrow)" />)}
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M700 106 V136 H450 V114" stroke-dasharray="6 5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      {['define task', 'get + split data', 'baseline', 'train', 'evaluate', 'iterate', 'deploy + monitor'].map((label, i) => (
        <text class="small" x={75 + i * 125} y="83" text-anchor="middle">{label}</text>
      ))}
      <text class="small" x="75" y="30" text-anchor="middle">and the metric</text>
      <text class="small" x="200" y="30" text-anchor="middle">train / val / test</text>
      <text class="small" x="325" y="30" text-anchor="middle">the dumb model</text>
      <text class="small" x="450" y="30" text-anchor="middle">the loop of Fig. 1</text>
      <text class="small" x="575" y="30" text-anchor="middle">on held-out data</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="575" y="160" text-anchor="middle">change one thing, train again</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Define the task and the metric.</strong> What is the input, what is the output, and what single number
      will tell you the model is good enough? The <span class="term">metric</span> is what the business cares about
      (accuracy, revenue, time saved) and is often different from the loss the optimizer minimizes. Decide it first,
      or you will decide it after seeing results, which is cheating.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Get the data and split it.</strong> Training, validation and test sets, separated before you look too
      closely. Clean it, and look at examples by hand; most project-ending bugs live in the data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Build a baseline.</strong> The simplest thing that could work: predict the average, predict the most
      common class, or use a rule of thumb. Every real model must beat the baseline on the validation set, and the
      margin by which it does so is the honest measure of what you have gained. The
      <a href="#baseline">next section</a> works this out in full.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Train.</strong> The loop of Fig. 1.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Evaluate on held-out data.</strong> The validation set during development. The test set once, at the
      end, for the number you report.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Iterate.</strong> Change one thing at a time: a feature, a model size, a hyperparameter. Measure again.
      Keep a log; the log is where the interview stories come from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deploy and monitor.</strong> The world changes after you ship, and so does the data. A model that was
      good in March can be bad in September. Monitoring is part of the job, and the production track in week 15 is
      about it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This is also the structure of <a href="/ai/projects/#ml-from-scratch">Project 0</a> and of every project after it.
    Projects that follow this shape produce numbers you can defend; projects that skip the baseline and the split
    produce numbers nobody should believe, including you.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-machine-learning"
    title="Kaggle Learn: Intro to Machine Learning"
    meta="A free, short, hands-on course that walks this exact workflow on a housing dataset in a notebook. About three hours in total; do the first four lessons this week."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Define the task and the evaluation metric, split the data before anything else, establish a baseline, train,
      evaluate on held-out data, iterate one change at a time, then deploy with monitoring. The metric is chosen up
      front; the test set is touched once.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="baseline">Baselines: is the model any good?</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A friend says their model predicts house prices with an error of 5,000. Is that good? You cannot tell. It depends
      on what you would get <em>without</em> a model. So before trusting any model, you ask: what score does the
      laziest possible guess get?
    </p>
    <p>
      Imagine a teacher who has to predict every student’s exam mark before reading anything about the students. The
      safest single guess is the class average: say it for everyone, and you are never wildly wrong. That lazy guess is
      a <strong>baseline</strong>. A real model reads the inputs (the house size, the student’s past marks), so it
      should beat the lazy guess. If it does not, the inputs taught it nothing, however impressive its own number
      looks.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    A <span class="term">baseline</span> is the simplest predictor that could reasonably be used, usually one that
    ignores the features entirely, against which every real model is compared. It turns a raw number (“MSE 3424”) into
    a meaningful one (“a third less error than guessing the average”). It also catches bugs: a model that does
    <em>worse</em> than the baseline is broken, not just weak.
  </p>

  <h3>The three houses, by hand</h3>
  <p>
    Go back to the three houses: sizes 1, 1.5 and 2 (thousand square feet), prices 200, 300 and 380. The baseline
    ignores the sizes and guesses the average price for every house:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\text{guess} = \frac{200 + 300 + 380}{3} = 293.3" />
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>House</th><th>Price</th><th>Baseline guess</th><th>Error</th><th>Squared</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1,000 sq ft</td><td>200</td><td>293.3</td><td>+93.3</td><td>8,711</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,500 sq ft</td><td>300</td><td>293.3</td><td>−6.7</td><td>44</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2,000 sq ft</td><td>380</td><td>293.3</td><td>−86.7</td><td>7,511</td></tr>
        <tr><td colspan="4"><strong>Mean squared error</strong></td><td><strong>5,422</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The best line, which <em>does</em> use the size, had a mean squared error of 22.2. Now the number means
    something: using the size removed almost all of the error the lazy guess made.
  </p>
  <Figure n={6} caption="Left: the baseline is a flat line. It gives every house the same price, so the errors (red) are large. Right: the best line uses the size and the errors almost vanish. The baseline is what the model has to beat.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 300" role="img" aria-label="Two plots of the three houses. Left: a flat line at 293 with long red error ticks. Right: the best-fit sloped line with tiny error ticks.">
      <g class="sketch">
        {[bl1, bl2].map((p) => <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${p.x} ${sy(p, 0)} H${p.x + p.w} M${sx(p, 0.5)} ${p.y} V${p.y + p.h}`} />)}
        <path class="stroke" d={line(bl1, 0, meanPrice)} stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="8 5"></path>
        {houses.map((h) => <path class="stroke ink-red" d={`M${sx(bl1, h.x)} ${sy(bl1, h.y)} V${sy(bl1, meanPrice)}`} stroke-width="2.5" />)}
        <path class="stroke" d={line(bl2, 180, 23.33)} stroke-width="2.5"></path>
        {houses.map((h) => <path class="stroke ink-red" d={`M${sx(bl2, h.x)} ${sy(bl2, h.y)} V${sy(bl2, 180 * h.x + 23.33)}`} stroke-width="2.5" />)}
        {houses.map((h) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(bl1, h.x)} cy={sy(bl1, h.y)} r="6" />)}
        {houses.map((h) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(bl2, h.x)} cy={sy(bl2, h.y)} r="6" />)}
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(bl1, 0.55)} y={sy(bl1, meanPrice) - 10}>guess 293 for all</text>
      <text x={bl1.x + bl1.w / 2} y="285" text-anchor="middle">baseline: MSE 5,422</text>
      <text x={bl2.x + bl2.w / 2} y="285" text-anchor="middle">best line: MSE 22.2</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(bl1, 0.5) - 6} y={bl1.y - 8} text-anchor="end">price</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(bl2, 0.5) - 6} y={bl2.y - 8} text-anchor="end">price</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <h3>One number for “how much better”</h3>
  <p>
    Compare the two errors with one ratio. The model’s error divided by the baseline’s error says what fraction of the
    lazy guess’s error is <em>left</em>. One minus that is the fraction the model removed:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\text{skill} = 1 - \frac{\text{model error}}{\text{baseline error}} = 1 - \frac{22.2}{5{,}422} = 0.996" />
  <p>
    Read it as “the model removes 99.6% of the error you would make by guessing the average”. Three things to know:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1 (100%)</strong> means a perfect model. <strong>0</strong> means no better than the baseline.</li>
    <li><strong>Below 0</strong> means worse than guessing the average. That is a bug or a badly broken model.</li>
    <li>
      When the error is mean squared error and the baseline is the mean, this number has a famous name,
      <Term id="r-squared">R²</Term> (“R-squared”). You will meet it properly in the
      <a href="/ai/notes/ml/linear-regression/#interpret">linear regression note</a>. It is nothing more than
      “improvement over the mean baseline”.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why the average, and not some other number?</h3>
  <p>
    If you must guess one number for everyone, which number is best depends on how mistakes are scored. There are
    two common ways. <strong>Mean squared error</strong> squares each miss and averages, as before. <strong>Mean
    absolute error</strong> (<span class="term">MAE</span>) just averages the sizes of the misses, ignoring whether
    each was too high or too low: misses of −20 and +10 give (20 + 10) / 2 = 15. Try four guesses on the three house
    prices, scored both ways:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Guess for every house</th><th>Mean squared error</th><th>Mean absolute error</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>250</td><td>7,300</td><td>76.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>293.3 (the mean)</strong></td><td><strong>5,422</strong> (smallest)</td><td>62.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>300 (the median)</strong></td><td>5,467</td><td><strong>60.0</strong> (smallest)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>350</td><td>8,633</td><td>76.7</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Squared error punishes big misses heavily, so the best single guess is pulled toward the far-away prices: that is
    the <strong>mean</strong>. Absolute error treats every unit of miss the same, so the best single guess is the
    middle value: the <strong>median</strong>. So the right baseline matches the metric: mean for squared error,
    median for absolute error. This is also why a single huge price in the data drags a mean-based model around while
    a median barely moves.
  </p>

  <h3>The usual baselines</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Task</th><th>Baseline</th><th>Why it is the right one</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Predict a number, scored by squared error</td><td>The training mean</td><td>The best single guess under squared error, as above.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Predict a number, scored by absolute error</td><td>The training median</td><td>The best single guess under absolute error.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pick a category</td><td>The most common class (the “majority class”)</td><td>The best single guess when you score by accuracy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Predict tomorrow from the past (a time series)</td><td>“Same as yesterday”, or same as this day last week</td><td>Hard to beat for things that change slowly, such as weather or daily sales.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Any task with a model already running</td><td>The current production model</td><td>A new model must beat what is already there, not just the lazy guess.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Any task</td><td>A simple rule a person would write, such as “flag emails containing ‘lottery’”</td><td>If machine learning cannot beat the obvious rule, it is not worth its cost.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Always compare against the <em>strongest</em> simple baseline, not the weakest. Guessing zero for every patient
    is technically a baseline, and any model beats it by miles, which proves nothing. The code below shows exactly
    this trap.
  </p>

  <h3>Code: the three houses</h3>
  <NewInCode label="New in this code: NumPy, the library every example uses" items={[
    ["import numpy as np", "Loads NumPy, the standard library for working with lists of numbers, under the short name np.", "numpy/import"],
    ["np.array([200.0, 300.0, 380.0])", "Turns a Python list into an array: a list of numbers that supports math on all of them at once.", "numpy/array"],
    ["baseline - prices, errors ** 2", "Math between arrays happens element by element: the first minus the first, the second minus the second, and so on. No loop needed.", "numpy/arith"],
    ["prices.mean(), np.mean(x)", "The average of all the numbers. Both spellings do the same thing.", "numpy/mean"],
    ["np.full_like(prices, guess)", "A new array the same size as prices, with every element set to guess: the same guess for every house.", "numpy/like"],
    ["np.median(prices)", "The middle value once the numbers are sorted.", "numpy/median"],
    ["np.abs(x)", "Drops the minus signs: the size of each number.", "numpy/abs"],
    ['f"{c:6.1f}"  and  f"{skill:.1%}"', "Print formats inside an f-string: :6.1f means one decimal, padded to 6 characters so columns line up; :.1% shows a fraction as a percentage.", "python/fstring-format"],
  ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={baselineHandCode} />

  <h3>Code: a real dataset, three baselines and a model</h3>
  <p>
    The diabetes dataset has 442 patients. For each one there are 10 measurements (age, body mass index, blood
    pressure and six blood tests) and a score for how much the disease progressed one year later, between 25 and 346.
    The code splits off 89 patients as a test set, scores three baselines and a linear model on them, and reports the
    skill.
  </p>
  <NewInCode label="New in this code: scikit-learn, the standard ML library" items={[
    ["from sklearn.datasets import load_diabetes", "scikit-learn (imported as sklearn) is the standard Python library for classical machine learning. You import each tool from the module that holds it.", "sklearn/import-sklearn"],
    ["X, y = load_diabetes(return_X_y=True)", "Loads a small real dataset that ships with the library and gives back two things: X, the table of measurements (one row per patient), and y, the answers.", "sklearn/toy-datasets"],
    ["train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=0)", "Shuffles the rows and keeps 20% aside as the test set. Returns four arrays in this order: X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test. random_state=0 makes the shuffle the same every run.", "sklearn/train-test-split"],
    ["LinearRegression()", "A model that fits the best straight line (with 10 features, the best flat surface) by least squares.", "sklearn/linear-regression"],
    [".fit(X_train, y_train) and .predict(X)", "fit is training: it sets the model's knobs from the training data. predict is inference: it uses those knobs to guess answers. fit gives back the model itself, so LinearRegression().fit(...) makes and trains it in one line.", "sklearn/fit-predict"],
    ["mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred)", "The average squared miss between the true answers and the predictions. True answers come first.", "sklearn/mse"],
    ["mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred)", "The average size of the misses, ignoring sign.", "sklearn/mae"],
    ["np.sqrt(x)", "The square root.", "numpy/sqrt"],
  ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={baselineDiabetesCode} />
  <p>How to read the output:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The mean baseline is the bar to clear.</strong> Guessing 151.6 for everyone gives a mean squared error of
      5,135, a typical miss of about 72 points.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The median wins on absolute error and loses on squared error</strong>, exactly as in the house table.
      The theory shows up even on real data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The zero baseline is the trap.</strong> Against an error of 28,913, the model looks like a miracle. It
      is not; zero is just a silly guess.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The model removes about a third of the baseline’s error</strong> (skill 33%), cutting the typical miss
      from about 72 to about 59 points. That is real but modest, and saying so honestly is what an interviewer wants
      to hear.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The baseline’s guess comes from the training answers</strong> (<code>y_train.mean()</code>), never the
      test answers. Using the test answers would let the baseline peek at the exam, the same cheating the test set
      exists to prevent.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>scikit-learn ships the lazy guess as a model of its own, so it slots into any pipeline:</p>
  <NewInCode items={[
    ['DummyRegressor(strategy="mean")', "A model that ignores the features and always predicts the training mean (or median, or a constant). The baseline, in the same fit / predict shape as every other model.", "sklearn/dummy"],
  ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={baselineDummyCode} />

  <h3>Code: when the baseline is already “95% accurate”</h3>
  <p>
    For categories the trap is sharper. When one class is rare, always guessing the common one already scores a high
    accuracy while doing nothing useful:
  </p>
  <NewInCode items={[
    ["rng = np.random.default_rng(0)", "Makes a random-number generator. The 0 is the seed: the same seed gives the same random numbers every run.", "numpy/default-rng"],
    ["rng.random(1000)", "1,000 random numbers between 0 and 1.", "numpy/rng-uniform"],
    ["draws < 0.05, baseline == is_spam", "Comparing an array gives an array of True / False, one per element.", "numpy/mask"],
    ["(a == 1) & (b == 1)", "& combines two True / False arrays element by element: True only where both are True.", "numpy/mask"],
    [".astype(int)", "Converts every element to another type; True becomes 1 and False becomes 0.", "numpy/astype"],
    ["x.sum(), np.sum(x)", "Adds up all the elements. On True / False it counts the Trues.", "numpy/sum"],
    ["np.zeros_like(is_spam)", "An array of zeros the same size as is_spam: “not spam” for every email.", "numpy/like"],
  ]} />
  <CodeBlock code={baselineSpamCode} />
  <p>
    So “our spam filter is 95% accurate” means nothing until you know the majority-class baseline is also about 95%.
    Week 3 covers the metrics that look at the rare class directly, called <Term id="precision-recall">precision and recall</Term>.
  </p>

  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#dummy-estimators"
    title="scikit-learn user guide: Dummy estimators"
    meta="A short section on DummyRegressor and DummyClassifier and their strategies. Five minutes."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Before training anything I build the simplest baseline: the training mean for regression, the majority class for
      classification, or ‘same as yesterday’ for a time series. I report the model as an improvement over that
      baseline, because a raw error number means nothing on its own. For mean squared error against the mean baseline,
      that improvement is exactly R². And I compare against the strongest simple baseline, not a straw man.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="vocabulary">The vocabulary, in one table</h2>
  <p>
    These are the words that will appear on every page from here on. Most confusion in interviews comes from using
    two of them interchangeably when they are not, so the right-hand column gives the pairs to keep apart.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Do not confuse with</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Example</span> (sample, instance, row)</td><td>One data point: one house, one email, one image.</td><td>A <em>feature</em>, which is one column, one fact about every example.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Feature</span></td><td>One measured input: size, word count, pixel value.</td><td>The <em>label</em>, which is the output we want to predict.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Label</span> (target, ground truth)</td><td>The correct answer for an example, used only in training and evaluation.</td><td>The <em>prediction</em>, which is the model’s guess at it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Parameters</span> (weights)</td><td>Numbers inside the model that training changes.</td><td><em>Hyperparameters</em>: settings you choose before training (model size, learning rate, number of epochs). Training never changes them; you tune them on the validation set.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Loss</span></td><td>The number the optimizer minimizes during training; must be smooth enough to optimize.</td><td>The <em>metric</em>: what you actually care about, often not smooth (accuracy, F1). You optimize the loss and report the metric.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Training</span> (fitting)</td><td>Running the loop to set the parameters.</td><td><em>Inference</em>: using the trained model to predict on new inputs. Different cost, different code path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Epoch</span></td><td>One full pass over the training data.</td><td>A <em>step</em> (iteration), one parameter update, usually on one <em>batch</em> of examples. An epoch is many steps.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Baseline</span></td><td>The simplest reference model, or a previous model, that a new one must beat.</td><td>A <em>benchmark</em>, which is a standard dataset and metric used to compare methods across papers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Generalization</span></td><td>Performance on data not seen in training.</td><td><em>Training performance</em>, which is only evidence of fitting, not of learning.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Language model</span> (LLM)</td><td>A model whose job is to guess the next piece of text. “Large” means billions of parameters.</td><td>A <em>chat assistant</em>, which is a language model plus extra training and a product around it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Token</span></td><td>One piece of text as the model sees it: usually a word or part of a word.</td><td>A <em>word</em>: long or rare words are several tokens; “unbelievable” might be “un”, “believ”, “able”.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Parameters are learned from data; hyperparameters are chosen beforehand and tuned on a validation set. The loss
      is what the optimizer minimizes; the metric is what we report. Training sets the parameters; inference uses
      them.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="nesting">AI, ML, deep learning, LLMs</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      These words nest like boxes. Artificial intelligence is the biggest box, anything that makes a computer do
      something that looks smart, including hand-written rules. Machine learning is the box inside it where the rules
      are learned from data. Deep learning is the box inside that where the model is a neural network: a model built
      from many layers of simple weighted sums, which week 5 opens up. Large language models are a corner of the deep
      learning box: very large networks trained on text to guess the next token.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={6} caption="Nested boxes. Everything in this track lives in the machine learning box; the deep learning and LLM tracks go deeper into the inner boxes. The four-part loop is the same in all of them.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 340" role="img" aria-label="Four nested rounded rectangles labelled artificial intelligence, machine learning, deep learning and large language models">
      <g class="sketch">
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="30" y="20" width="660" height="300" rx="14"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="120" y="70" width="540" height="220" rx="12"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-green" x="230" y="120" width="400" height="140" rx="10"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-blue" x="360" y="165" width="262" height="60" rx="8"></rect>
      </g>
      <text x="50" y="52">artificial intelligence</text>
      <text class="small" x="50" y="308">includes hand-written rules, search, planning</text>
      <text x="140" y="100">machine learning</text>
      <text class="small" x="140" y="280">rules learned from data: this track</text>
      <text x="250" y="150">deep learning</text>
      <text class="small" x="250" y="250">multi-layer neural networks: weeks 5–6</text>
      <text x="380" y="190">large language models</text>
      <text class="small" x="380" y="214">huge networks trained on text: weeks 7+</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    The reason to be precise about the boxes is that interviewers use them to probe depth. “Deep learning” is not a
    different kind of learning; it is machine learning with a particular, very flexible model and the same four parts.
    “Classical” machine learning, the subject of this track, refers to the models that are not deep networks: linear
    models, trees, nearest neighbours, clustering. They still win on most tabular business data, they train in
    seconds on a laptop, and they are what interviewers reach for when they want to check that you understand the
    fundamentals rather than only the fashion.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Machine learning is the subset of AI where behaviour is learned from data; deep learning is the subset of ML
      using multi-layer neural networks; LLMs are very large neural networks trained self-supervised on text.
      Classical ML still dominates tabular problems and is the right baseline before reaching for a network.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

