  <h2 id="later">Where you will meet this again</h2>
  <Callout kind="note" label="Optional preview">
    <p>
      Find the four parts in a real language model (GPT-2, from 2019) and the picture stays the same; only the sizes
      change. Words from later weeks link to the glossary. Skip this on a first read if you like.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data.</strong> About 40 GB of web text. Each training example is a window of 1,024 tokens, and the label at each position is simply the next token. No human labelled anything: self-supervised.</li>
    <li><strong>Model.</strong> A particular kind of neural network called a <Term id="transformer">transformer</Term>, with 124 million parameters (small) up to 1.5 billion (large). A function from a token sequence to a probability for each possible next token, with knobs.</li>
    <li><strong>Loss.</strong> The <Term id="log-loss">log loss</Term>: minus the logarithm of the probability the model gave to the token that actually came next, averaged over all positions. The <a href="/ai/notes/math/functions-exponents-logs/">next note</a> builds it; week 4 gives it its grown-up name, <Term id="cross-entropy">cross-entropy</Term>.</li>
    <li><strong>Optimizer.</strong> A polished variant of gradient descent called <Term id="adam">Adam</Term> (week 6), run for hundreds of thousands of steps, each on a batch of sequences.</li>
    <li><strong>Hyperparameters.</strong> Number of layers, width, learning rate, batch size, how many tokens the model sees at once. Chosen by people, before training, and reported in the paper.</li>
    <li><strong>Inference.</strong> Run the trained model on a prompt, get a probability for each next token, pick one, append it, repeat. That is “generation”.</li>
    <li><strong>Generalization.</strong> Measured as loss on held-out text the model never trained on; a model that memorized its training set would have low training loss and useless outputs on anything new, exactly the wiggly curve of Fig. 3.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fine-tuning, RAG and agents, the subjects of the later tracks, are ways of adapting or wrapping this one trained
    function. Keep locating the four parts and none of it will feel like magic.
  </p>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The question usually comes as “explain machine learning to me as if I were not technical, then as if I were”.
    Here is both, in about ninety seconds.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “Plainly: in normal programming a person writes the rules; in machine learning we show the computer many
      examples with the right answers and it adjusts its own internal settings until its answers match. The rules end
      up inside those settings. We check that it learned something real, rather than memorizing, by testing it on
      examples it never saw.
    </p>
    <p>
      Precisely: every ML system has four parts. Training data, pairs of features and labels. A model, which is a
      parameterized function from features to a prediction. A loss function, a single number measuring how wrong the
      predictions are, such as mean squared error for regression or the log loss for classification. And an
      optimizer, usually a form of gradient descent, that adjusts the parameters to minimize the loss. Training
      iterates that loop; inference applies the learned parameters to new inputs. The actual objective is
      generalization, performance on held-out data, so we split off a test set before training and tune any
      hyperparameters, the settings we choose rather than learn, on a separate validation set. Supervised learning
      has human labels; unsupervised learning finds structure without them; language-model training is
      self-supervised, because the next token is the label and the loss is an ordinary supervised one.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Give an example of a hyperparameter and how you would choose it.” Learning rate; try several values, pick the one with the best validation loss, never the test loss.</li>
    <li>“Loss versus metric?” Loss is what you can optimize (smooth); the metric is what you report (accuracy, F1). They should agree in direction, but they are different numbers.</li>
    <li>“Why not evaluate on the training data?” It measures memorization, not learning. The wiggly curve has zero training error and is useless.</li>
    <li>“Is deep learning a different kind of ML?” No; same four parts with a neural network as the model.</li>
    <li>“What would you do first on a new problem?” Define the metric, split the data, build the dumbest baseline, then try the simplest model that could work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="What are the four parts of every machine-learning system, and what does each do?">
    <p>Data (examples to learn from), model (a parameterized function from features to predictions), loss (a number measuring how wrong the predictions are), optimizer (adjusts the parameters to reduce the loss). Training iterates the loop.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter? Give two examples of each.">
    <p>Parameters are learned by the optimizer from data: the slope and intercept of a line, the weights of a network. Hyperparameters are chosen before training and tuned on validation data: learning rate, number of layers, number of epochs, regularization strength.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why is the training loss not the goal?">
    <p>The goal is generalization: good predictions on unseen data. Training loss can be driven to zero by memorizing, which does not generalize. Held-out data is the only honest measure.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Define regression and classification in one sentence each. What changes in the training loop between them?">
    <p>Regression predicts a continuous number; classification predicts a category. Only the model’s last step and the loss change: squared error for regression, the log loss for classification. Data, optimizer and loop are the same.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is training a language model supervised or unsupervised?">
    <p>Self-supervised: the mechanics are supervised (a label and a loss at every position) but the labels are the next tokens of the text itself, so no human labelling is needed.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is a baseline and why do you build one first?">
    <p>The simplest reference predictor, such as the training mean or the majority class. Without it you cannot tell whether a model’s score is good; the gain over the baseline is the real measure of what the model learned.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Three apartments rent for 10, 12 and 20 (thousand a year). Compute the mean baseline and its mean squared error, and the median baseline and its mean absolute error.">
    <p>
      Mean = 14. Errors −4, −2, +6; squared 16, 4, 36; MSE = 56/3 ≈ 18.7. Median = 12. Absolute errors 2, 0, 8;
      MAE = 10/3 ≈ 3.3. (The mean’s MAE would be 12/3 = 4, worse; the median’s MSE would be 68/3 ≈ 22.7, worse.)
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="A model has test MSE 400; the mean baseline has 500. What is its skill? Another model has MSE 550. What does that tell you?">
    <p>
      Skill = 1 − 400/500 = 0.2: it removes 20% of the baseline’s error. The second model has skill 1 − 550/500 = −0.1,
      worse than guessing the average: something is broken (a bug, a leak in reverse, a bad feature scale), not merely weak.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the difference between the loss and the metric?">
    <p>The loss is the smooth quantity the optimizer minimizes (MSE, cross-entropy). The metric is what the task cares about and what you report (accuracy, F1, revenue), often not differentiable. You optimize the loss and monitor the metric.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What are the training, validation and test sets for?">
    <p>Training: fitting parameters. Validation: choosing hyperparameters and models during development. Test: one final, untouched estimate of generalization. Tuning on the test set makes its number meaningless.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is an epoch? How does it differ from a step?">
    <p>An epoch is one full pass over the training data. A step is one parameter update, usually on one batch. An epoch of 10,000 examples with batch size 100 is 100 steps.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Name a task for each: supervised regression, supervised classification, unsupervised learning, self-supervised learning, reinforcement learning.">
    <p>House price prediction; spam detection; customer clustering with k-means; next-token prediction for an LLM; learning to play a game from win/loss rewards (or RLHF for a chat model).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Explain the relationship between AI, machine learning, deep learning and LLMs.">
    <p>Nested subsets: ML is the part of AI where behaviour is learned from data; deep learning is ML with multi-layer neural networks; LLMs are large transformers trained self-supervised on text. All share the four-part loop.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="A model has training MSE 10 and test MSE 400. What do you suspect, and what is one thing you would try?">
    <p>Overfitting: it fits the training data far better than new data. Try more data, a simpler model, regularization, or check for a data leak between the sets. The overfitting note covers the options.</p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first, then check. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. For the three houses (1, 200), (1.5, 300), (2, 380), compute the MSE of the line ŷ = 160x + 60 and of ŷ = 180x + 20. Which is better?">
    <p>
      First: predictions 220, 300, 380; errors 20, 0, 0; MSE 400/3 = 133.3. Second: 200, 290, 380; errors 0, −10, 0;
      MSE 100/3 = 33.3. The second. Neither is the least-squares optimum, which is <Math tex="w = 180, b = 23.3" /> with
      MSE 22.2; the linear regression note derives it.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Classify each as a parameter or a hyperparameter: the slope of a regression line; the number of trees in a ‘random forest’ model (week 4); the learning rate; the intercept b of a line; the number of layers in a network; the entries of a model’s lookup table of word vectors.">
    <p>Parameter, hyperparameter, hyperparameter, parameter, hyperparameter, parameter.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Classify each task: predicting tomorrow’s temperature; deciding whether a transaction is fraud; grouping news articles by topic with no labels; predicting the masked word in a sentence; choosing ad placements to maximize clicks over time.">
    <p>Supervised regression; supervised classification; unsupervised clustering; self-supervised; reinforcement learning (a bandit problem).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. A dataset has 1,000 rows and 12 columns, one of which is the label. How many examples, how many features? If the training set is 80% and batch size is 50, how many steps per epoch?">
    <p>1,000 examples, 11 features. 800 training examples / 50 = 16 steps per epoch.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. Write down a sensible baseline for each: house prices; spam detection where 5% of email is spam; next-token prediction.">
    <p>Predict the mean (or median) training price. Predict “not spam” for everything (95% accuracy, which shows why accuracy is a poor metric here). Predict the most frequent token, or a unigram frequency model.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. You tuned the learning rate by trying ten values and picking the one with the best test accuracy. What is wrong, and what should you have done?">
    <p>The test set has been used for a choice, so its accuracy is now optimistically biased and no longer an honest estimate. Tune on a validation set (or cross-validation) and touch the test set once at the end.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. Describe the four parts for a spam filter that outputs a probability of spam.">
    <p>Data: emails with spam/not-spam labels, turned into feature lists (word counts). Model: a weighted sum of the word counts, squashed into a probability between 0 and 1 (week 3 calls this logistic regression). Loss: the log loss, which charges a lot for being confidently wrong (next note). Optimizer: gradient descent on the weights and the bias.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. Describe the four parts for training a language model such as GPT-2.">
    <p>Data: web text cut into 1,024-token windows, label = the next token at every position. Model: a very large neural network (124 million parameters) that outputs a probability for each possible next token. Loss: the log loss of the true next token, averaged over positions. Optimizer: a variant of gradient descent. Self-supervised because the labels come from the data.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. Explain, in two sentences a product manager would accept, why the model that scored 99% on the training data might be worthless.">
    <p>It may have memorized the training examples instead of learning the pattern, like a student who memorized last year’s exam. The only score that counts is on examples it has never seen, and we have not measured that yet.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. Sketch the training loop of Fig. 1 from memory and label which arrow carries the gradient.">
    <p>Data → model → prediction → loss (with the label entering the loss); loss → optimizer → model. The arrow from the optimizer back to the model carries the update, computed from the gradient of the loss with respect to the parameters.</p>
  </QA>
